    "md5": "7e3a3c0019dbbd147a3fb147ab6ac7e2",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1657,
    "chunk_text": "\u201c\u2026.. I am accused of having attempted to sabotage the Ja-Ela by-election campaign by issuing a press statement regarding the Ward Place incident. At the very outset, I must point out that my statement did not blame any individual or the party for this unfortunate incident. My constituents from the Dedigama electorate were brutally assaulted when they came to visit Mr. J.R. Jayewardene, to speak on my behalf. My statement condemned this disgusting act \u2026\u2026\u201d\n\n\u0db8\u0dad\u0dd4\u0db8\u0dcf\u0dc1\u0dda \u0daf\u0dd0\u0daf\u0dd2\u0d9c\u0db8 \u0db4\u0dcf\u0d9a\u0dca\u0dc2\u0dd2\u0dd9\u0dba\u0db1\u0dca\u0da7 \u0db4\u0dc4\u0dba\u0daf\u0dd3\u0db8 \u0dc3\u0db8\u0dca\u0db6\u0db1\u0dca\u0db0\u0dc1\u0dba\u0db1\u0dca \u0d85\u0db4\u0dca\u200d\u0dbb\u0dc3\u0dcf\u0daf\u0dba \u0db4\u0dca\u200d\u0dbb\u0dd9\u0dcf \u0dd9\u0dba\u0db1 \u0db8\u0dd9 \u0dad\u0db8\u0dd2. \u0d92 \u0dd9\u0dc1\u0dc5\u0dda. \u0d8a\u0dc5\u0d9f\u0da7 \u0d9a\u0dd2\u0dba\u0db1\u0dc0\u0dcf \u0dc1\u0db8\u0dc1\u0dc4\u0db8:\n\n\u0db8\u0dad\u0dc1\u0dd9\u0ddc\u0da7 \u0dc1\u0db8\u0dca\u0dd9 \u0dad\u0db8\u0dd2. \u0d85\u0db1\u0dd2\u0d9a\u0dca \u0db4\u0dd0\u0dad\u0dca\u0dad. \u0dc1\u0db8\u0dad\u0dd0\u0db1 \u0dd9\u0dcf\u0dba\u0da7\u0dcf \u0dad\u0dd4\u0db1\u0d9a\u0dca \u0daf\u0dd3\u0dbd\u0dcf \u0dad\u0dd2\u0dc1\u0db6\u0db1\u0dc0\u0dcf. \u0dad\u0dd4\u0db1\u0dca\u0dc1\u0dc0\u0db1\u0dd2 \u0dd9\u0dcf\u0dba\u0da7\u0dcf\u0dc0 \u0dc1\u0db8\u0dca\u0dd9\u0dd2..\n\n\"Thirdly, I am accused of handing an affidavit and a letter to the Minister of Finance. When this accusation first appeared in the \u201cjournal\u201d of 21st February, 1976, I wrote to Mr. J.R. Jayewardene on 2nd March, 1976, denying the charge. When written explanation was requested from me I wrote to him on 6th March, 1976\u2026..\u201d \u0d9a\u0dd2\u0dba\u0dbd\u0dcf \u0d94\u0dd4\u0dad \u0d92 \u0dd9\u0dcf\u0dcf\u0dc0 \u0d9a\u0dd2\u0dba\u0db1\u0dc0\u0dcf.\n\nThen, Sir, very interestingly, he says what the reason is. I further quote:\n\n\u201cProblems between Mr. J.R. Jayewardene and myself arose not because of the Colombo South by-election as publicised, but because of the Kalawewa by-election.\"\n\nThere were other accusations that were made against him, Sir."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_hansard_2020s",
    "doc_id": "2025-01-24-2025-01-24",
    "num": "2025-01-24",
    "date_str": "2025-01-24",
    "description": "Hansard of January 24, 2025",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.parliament.lk/en/business-of-parliament/hansards",
    "lang": "si-ta-en",
    "url_pdf": "https://www.parliament.lk/uploads/documents/hansard/1738563571096592.pdf",
    "chunk_id": "2025-01-24-2025-01-24-0053",
    "chunk_index": 53,
    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "175cc602e2ca020d17442e40c6653a20",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1919,
    "chunk_text": "\u201cProblems between Mr. J.R. Jayewardene and myself arose not because of the Colombo South by-election as publicised, but because of the Kalawewa by-election.\"\n\nThere were other accusations that were made against him, Sir.\n\nAnd he ends by saying,\n\n\u201cInsinuations are being made that I am working with the Government. Let it be known that I have no intention of joining the Government or any other political party. It is my intention to place the facts before the people and build up a force to halt the dictatorial trend in the UNP. What I intend creating may with the passage of time erupt into a political force, depending upon the wishes of the people and I leave it to the people to decide.\u201d\n\nSo, finally, what happened obviously was that Parliamentary Elections of 1977 came up and J.R. Jayewardene won with a landslide five-sixth majority. The Hon. Rukman Senanayake did not contest, but subsequently came to Parliament in 1994 and I am not going to repeat that recent history.\n\nThe point I am going to make is how the Hon. Rukman Senanayake stood up and fought for what he thought was the right thing to do at that time and he fought against the dictatorial leadership of the late Hon. J. R. Jayewardene at that time. And, the late Hon. J. R. Jayewardene, being the cunning fox he was, as everybody referred to him \"Yankee Dickey\", found a way to take care of his political opponents by creating a smoke and mirrors situation and accusing them of various things. I think the lesson here for all of us is that politics is a very difficult profession to be in. You all are new and you will also learn fairly quickly. It is tough to stand up for what you really believe in. I think what people admired about the Hon. Rukman Senanayake was the fact that he always stood his ground. He said, \"whether I win, lose or whether I would not even contest, I am going to stick to what I think are the right things to do\"."
  },
